\title{Public-key cryptography}
\date{2026-07-17T11:41:57Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{There is a different idea known as a \em{public-key} cryptosystem which does not have these drawbacks. We’ll start by outlining the general properties of such a system.

\ul{
  \li{Everyone who wants to communicate picks their own personal pair
  of keys: a \em{private key} and a \em{public key}.}
  \li{The private key should be kept absolutely, 100\% secret.}
  \li{The public key should be published as widely as possible.  You
  should put your public key on your website, in a public directory of
  public keys (like a phone book), and so on.}
  \li{In order to send a message, the sender looks up the public key
  of the recipient and uses it to encode the message.}
  \li{The recipient decodes the message using their \em{private} key.}
}
}

\p{In order for this to make sense the system should have a few
properties:
\ul{
  \li{A public and private key work together so that messages
  encrypted using a public key can \em{only} be decrypted using the
  corresponding private key.}
  \li{It should be impossible to figure out someone's corresponding
  private key if you only know their public key.}
}
}

\p{It is not at all obvious that such a scheme is even possible!  But it
\em{is} possible using some clever mathematics.}
